## 8. Fillers — very US (natural, not "lazy")

*"Well… you know… I mean…"*

| Phrase | Function | Dev example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Well…** | soft opener, buying time | *"**Well,** it's not that simple."* |
| **You know** | shared context | *"It's the usual caching issue, **you know.**"* |
| **I mean** | clarify / restart sentence | *"It's broken. **I mean,** it works, but slowly."* |
| **Kind of / Sort of** | hedge, uncertainty | *"It's **kind of** working — pass on iOS, fail on Android."* |
| **Right?** | confirm | *"We agreed on Redux for auth, **right?**"* |
| **You see** | explain reasoning | *"**You see,** the cache was stale when the user returned."* |

**Insight:** fillers are what makes speech sound **native**, not "uneducated". Non-native speakers avoid them and end up sounding robotic. Sprinkle 1–2 per 30 seconds of speech.

---

## 9. Conclusion / Closing

*"So, anyway, at the end of the day…"*

| Phrase | Function | Dev example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **So** | most common closer | *"**So,** that's my update."* |
| **Anyway** | move on from digression | *"**Anyway,** let's get back to the API design."* |
| **In the end** | final outcome | *"**In the end,** we went with the simpler approach."* |
| **At the end of the day** | pragmatic summary | *"**At the end of the day,** the user just wants it to load fast."* |
| **To wrap up** | meeting close (formal) | *"**To wrap up** — we ship Friday, QA Thursday."* |
| **That's about it** | casual close | *"**That's about it** from me."* |

**Senior move:** closing a meeting with *"To wrap up — decision, owner, deadline."* — this single habit will make you look 2 years more senior.

---

## 10. The real American speech pattern

American devs **do not** use fancy words. They repeat the same core set constantly:

> **so, but, like, actually, just, kind of, you know, I mean**

Natural-sounding sentence:

> *"I was tired, **but** I **still** went out, **you know**, **just** to relax **a bit**."*

Or dev version:

> *"I was debugging all morning, **but** I **still** couldn't repro it, **you know**, **kind of** intermittent, **so** I filed a ticket."*

Target: **sprinkle** fillers + emphasis naturally. Don't try to speak "polished" English — that sounds like a news anchor, not a dev.

